{"type":"text","timestamp":1777734592963,"sessionID":"ses_216c60402ffe2JsfyY5wEgPCMY","part":{"id":"prt_de93cb9ca002oYvi7Y9L6Y93vK","messageID":"msg_de93cb49900151a11irK7yKYNX","sessionID":"ses_216c60402ffe2JsfyY5wEgPCMY","type":"text","text":"Wait, there's still a problem: when a contains only 3 elements but they're NOT the three largest ranks, sort_small_a sorts them locally but won't result in a globally sorted a after the pa phase.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The pa phase picks max of b and pa's it. After all b is emptied, stack a has: originally sorted 3-element suffix + pa'd elements in decreasing order of value. So final a top→bot = smallest pa'd, ..., largest pa'd, then original 3-element block.\n\nFor correctness: the 3 elements left in a MUST have the three largest ranks (n-3, n-2, n-1) and be in sorted order (top=n-3, bottom=n-1). Then pa of rank n-4, n-5, ... ends with top=0.\n\nSo I need to guarantee that when we stop the push phase, a has exactly ranks {n-3, n-2, n-1}. My chunk loop only advances when no current-chunk ranks remain in a. That should correctly leave behind only the highest chunk elements — but my loop stops at `a.len() > 3`, so it might leave fewer than 3 in the last chunk or more.\n\nLet me rework: the last chunk may have fewer than 3 elements (if chunk_size < 3). Better: stop pushing when a.len() == 3 OR when we've advanced past the last chunk that contains the top 3 ranks.\n\nActually the simplest fix: push everything to b except the 3 largest ranks. Specifically, chunks only cover ranks 0..n-3, and the last 3 ranks stay in a.","time":{"start":1777734564298,"end":1777734592962}}}
